---
import DocLayout from "../layouts/DocLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
---

<DocLayout title="Security" description="What the library guarantees, and the one place it does not.">
  <h1>Security</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A library that injects UI into your page has an obligation about what it will and will not do
    with your data. Here is the exact position.
  </p>

  <h2>Guarantees</h2>

  <p>
    These are enforced by lint rules and a dependency audit that runs in CI, not merely documented:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No <code>innerHTML</code></strong> outside one explicitly named module, and that
      module is unreachable unless you opt in.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No <code>eval</code> or <code>Function</code></strong> anywhere in
      <code>src/</code>. Configuration strings are never executed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No network access.</strong> No <code>fetch</code>, no <code>XMLHttpRequest</code>, no
      <code>sendBeacon</code>. Icons are local path data; nothing is fetched from a CDN.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No analytics, no telemetry, no hidden requests</strong> of any kind.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No prototype or global mutation.</strong> No patching of built-in prototypes, no
      unrequested <code>window</code> properties, no overwriting of globals.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No reading of application data.</strong> Form values, input content and text are
      never collected.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No selector injection.</strong> Target identifiers are compared as exact string values,
      so nothing from your configuration reaches the CSS parser.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Complete cleanup.</strong> <code>destroy()</code> removes every listener, observer,
      timer and node the tour created.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <Callout title="Enforced, not promised">
    <p>
      <code>npm run check:deps</code> fails the build if <code>packages/core</code> gains a runtime
      dependency, or if the source contains <code>innerHTML</code>, <code>eval</code>,
      <code>fetch</code> or <code>XMLHttpRequest</code> outside the single allowlisted module.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>How text is rendered</h2>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`createTour({
  tourId: "x",
  steps: [{ tourId: "a", title: "<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>", content: "<script>…<\/script>" }],
});`}
  />

  <p>The markup is displayed as visible text. Nothing is parsed, nothing executes:</p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="text"
    code={`<h2 class="tat-tooltip__title">&lt;img src=x onerror=alert(1)&gt;</h2>`}
  />

  <p>
    Every piece of ordinary text — titles, content, labels, progress — goes through
    <code>textContent</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>The one exception: <code>unsafeHTML</code></h2>

  <p>
    There is one documented way to render authored markup. It is off by default, named for what it
    is, and it is the only <code>innerHTML</code> in the package.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`createTour({
  tourId: "x",
  unsafeHTML: true, // opt in, per tour
  steps: [
    {
      tourId: "a",
      content: "Plain text as usual.",
      unsafeContent: "<p>Rich <strong>authored</strong> markup.</p>",
    },
  ],
});`}
  />

  <Callout title="Treat this exactly as you would treat innerHTML" variant="danger">
    <p>
      When enabled, <code>unsafeContent</code> is parsed as markup. It can load remote resources and,
      in some document contexts, execute script. It is equivalent in risk to assigning to
      <code>innerHTML</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Never</strong> enable it for user input, API responses, URL parameters or anything you
      did not author yourself.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <p>
    The library cannot enforce where the string came from. What it does enforce is that you had to
    say <code>unsafeHTML: true</code> out loud, in the tour's own configuration.
  </p>

  <h2>Custom callbacks</h2>

  <p>
    <code>on.*</code> callbacks, <code>when</code> predicates and action handlers are functions you
    supply. They run in your page with full access to whatever the page can do — that is the nature
    of a callback.
  </p>

  <p>What the library guarantees about them:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>A throwing callback cannot corrupt the engine — it is caught and reported via <code>error</code>.</li>
    <li>A rejecting callback is awaited and its rejection caught, so ordering still holds.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Target resolution safety</h2>

  <p>
    The obvious way to find <code>data-tour-id="home.cta"</code> is to escape the identifier and
    build a selector. This library does not, because that mixes CSS identifier escaping with CSS
    string escaping — and getting it wrong is a syntax error at best, a wrong element at worst.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`// Instead: a constant selector, then exact comparison.
const candidates = root.querySelectorAll("[data-tour-id]");
for (const element of candidates) {
  if (element.getAttribute("data-tour-id") === id) matches.push(element);
}`}
  />

  <p>
    So <code>'a"b'</code>, <code>a\\b</code>, <code>100%</code>, <code>[data-x]</code> and
    <code>*</code> all resolve as literal values. There is nothing to escape, so there is nothing to
    get wrong.
  </p>

  <p>
    The one place a selector is accepted is <code>target.selector</code> — which <em>you</em> wrote,
    in code. It is validated, and an invalid one produces a clear error rather than a silent miss.
  </p>

  <h2>Persistence and privacy</h2>

  <p>
    Disabled by default. When enabled with the <code>localStorage</code> adapter, exactly three
    fields are written under <code>take-a-tour:&#123;tourId&#125;</code>: <code>tourId</code>,
    <code>stepIndex</code> and <code>completed</code>. Storage failures are caught and degrade to
    memory, so a blocked-storage error never becomes a user-visible crash.
  </p>

  <h2>Reporting a vulnerability</h2>

  <p>
    Please report suspected vulnerabilities privately rather than opening a public issue. See
    <code>SECURITY.md</code> in the repository.
  </p>
</DocLayout>